-- title:
--   Commutant of a simple commuting representation is a field acting simply transitively
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, $\Gamma$ a group and $V$ a nonzero $k$-vector space, and let $\rho : \Gamma \to \mathrm{GL}_k(V)$ be a $k$-linear representation, regarded as a map $\Gamma \to \operatorname{End}_k(V)$. Assume (i) $\rho$ is simple in the sense that every $k$-submodule $W \subseteq V$ with $\rho(g)v \in W$ for all $g \in \Gamma$ and all $v \in W$ equals $\bot$ or $\top$, and (ii) the operators commute, $\rho(a)\rho(b) = \rho(b)\rho(a)$ for all $a, b \in \Gamma$. Write $C$ for the centralizer, as a $k$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_k(V)$, of the set $\rho(\Gamma) = \operatorname{range}(\rho)$. The conclusion is a conjunction of three assertions: first, $C$ coincides with the $k$-subalgebra $k[\rho(\Gamma)]$ generated by $\rho(\Gamma)$; second, $C$ satisfies `IsField`, i.e. it is nontrivial, its multiplication is commutative, and each of its nonzero elements is invertible in $C$; third, for every $v \in V$ with $v \neq 0$ the evaluation map $C \to V$, $\varphi \mapsto \varphi(v)$, is bijective. Thus $V$ is a line over the field $C$.
--
--   This is Schur's lemma for a simple representation whose operators pairwise commute, sharpened by the double-centralizer computation identifying the commutant with the algebra generated by the operators and exhibiting $V$ as a one-dimensional vector space over that field; consequently $\Gamma$ acts through a character with values in $C^\times$. It is used in the analysis of the inertia action on finite flat group schemes and in the construction of a quotient line for representations of $p$-groups, via [`HopfAlgebra.exists_field_lineAction_of_finite_flat_of_inertiaSimple_step`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.exists_field_lineAction_of_finite_flat_of_inertiaSimple_step) and [`Representation.exists_submodule_quotient_line_of_commutator_le_of_isPGroup`](thm.html#Representation.exists_submodule_quotient_line_of_commutator_le_of_isPGroup).
